md -- guest and hypervisor machine descriptions
===============================================

    make md                 legion
    make md TARGET=pci   pci
    make md TARGET=all   both, one after the other

Output: $(OUTDIR)/1up-md.bin (guest) and 1up-hv.bin (hypervisor). With
TARGET=all each config goes to its own $(OUTDIR)/<target>/ instead.

Layout
------
    md/common.pdesc, common.hdesc   macros and constants only
    md/legion/1up.{pdesc,hdesc}     the legion machine, complete
                                    (+ config.mk, README)
    md/pci/1up.{pdesc,hdesc}        the pci machine, complete
                                    (+ config.mk, README)

Each machine directory has its own README (device list, matching QEMU command
line, what is known to boot) and its own config.mk (the OBP target and the
MD_LEGION_IO/MD_PCI flags used by `make config` and the rebuild stamp).

Each variant is self-contained, with no conditional compilation. The two
overlap on purpose, the same way Sun's own configs do. The cost: a change
meant for both machines has to be made twice.

TARGET is the directory name. config.mk discovers valid names by wildcard
(any md/<target>/ with a 1up.pdesc) rather than listing them, and -include's
that name's config.mk for the coupling -- so config.mk itself never names a
config, and adding one is just adding a directory: 1up.pdesc, 1up.hdesc,
config.mk, README. No top-level file needs editing.

"all" is not a directory: it is a request to build every discovered
md/<target>/ in one pass, each into its own $(OUTDIR)/<target> by re-invoking
make.

What OBP takes from here
------------------------
OBP's configuration comes from this MD, not from NVRAM. These nodes are the
only place to change it:

    node variables    boot-device, input-device, output-device, auto-boot?,
                      fcode-debug?
    node devalias     the table `devalias` prints at ok

pci aliases:

    net    /pci@0/network@0                     e1000 (Ophir FCode)
    disk   /pci@0/ide@2/disk@0                   cmd646 IDE, primary master
    disk0  /pci@0/ide@2/disk@0                   same unit
    cdrom  /pci@0/ide@2/cdrom@1,0                cmd646 IDE, primary slave

legion aliases:

    disk   /virtual-devices/disk@0              the Legion disk
    vdisk  /virtual-devices/disk@0              same device

Both also carry nvram, ttya, ttyb, and md-type -- a label naming the variant,
which `devalias` prints.

Where the hypervisor MD's values come from
------------------------------------------
Names used below: T1-archive is OpenSPARCT1_Arch.1.5.tar.bz2, T2-archive is
OpenSPARCT2_Arch.1.2.tar.bz2 (md/REFERENCE, Sources).

md/*/1up.hdesc is written to what the hypervisor reads; the sources are in
src/hypervisor/src (BSD+). The layout is that of the MD sources in T2-archive
(t1_fpga/src/xilinx/configs/*_hv.desc; no licence named for them). Of the 22
properties of the legion hypervisor MD, 11 have the same value there (pci: 13
of 24).

md/REFERENCE lists every node and property of the hypervisor MD: its value,
what reads it (file:line), and whether and how the two archives have it. The
same is written value by value in the comments of md/*/1up.hdesc.

No specification describes this MD. FWARC/2005/115 and the Hypervisor API
(8.23) describe the guest MD (there content-version is the string "1", and
cfg-handle of virtual-devices and channel-devices is only "a unique 64-bit
value"); FWARC/2006/700 and 2007/138 describe the PRI. The first-hand sources
for this file are the hypervisor's own code.

Editing
-------
Two limits are worth knowing before you add anything.

The guest MD must stay under 8 KB. QEMU loads it into a fixed-size ROM with no
bounds check, so a larger file is truncated and the machine boots to nothing at
all, with no message.

A PCI device that raises INTB, INTC or INTD needs its own interrupt-map-entry.
There is no fallback and no warning if one is missing -- the device's driver
just times out. The node format is FWARC 2007/386,
https://sun4v.github.io/ARChive/FWARC/2007/386/
